1
00:00:00,375 --> 00:00:02,000
One tiny bone helped reveal

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,000
an entire human lineage.

3
00:00:04,475 --> 00:00:05,975
A finger-bone fragment from

4
00:00:05,975 --> 00:00:08,225
Denisova Cave, in Siberia,

5
00:00:08,225 --> 00:00:10,562
contained ancient DNA.

6
00:00:10,562 --> 00:00:12,300
When researchers analyzed it,

7
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:13,925
the genetic evidence pointed

8
00:00:13,925 --> 00:00:15,912
to a previously unrecognized

9
00:00:15,912 --> 00:00:18,525
archaic group: the Denisovans.

10
00:00:19,150 --> 00:00:20,950
Usually, a fossil's appearance

11
00:00:20,950 --> 00:00:22,300
starts the argument about its

12
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:24,987
relatives. Here, genetics opened

13
00:00:24,987 --> 00:00:26,225
a different door.

14
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:28,800
Later fossils and genetic findings

15
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:30,562
expanded the story.

16
00:00:30,562 --> 00:00:32,538
Denisovan-related ancestry

17
00:00:32,538 --> 00:00:34,575
is not confined to one cave

18
00:00:34,575 --> 00:00:37,625
or one identical ancient population.

19
00:00:38,175 --> 00:00:40,238
But DNA does not hand us a

20
00:00:40,238 --> 00:00:41,438
photograph.

21
00:00:41,438 --> 00:00:42,962
Confident-looking Denisovan

22
00:00:42,962 --> 00:00:44,812
faces in illustrations are

23
00:00:44,812 --> 00:00:46,800
artistic reconstructions.

24
00:00:47,225 --> 00:00:48,925
The discovery is remarkable

25
00:00:48,925 --> 00:00:50,788
without inventing their appearance

26
00:00:50,788 --> 00:00:52,400
or personal lives.

27
00:00:52,850 --> 00:00:54,638
A branch of the human family

28
00:00:54,638 --> 00:00:56,200
can be difficult to recognize

29
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:58,475
in bones, yet leave evidence

30
00:00:58,475 --> 00:01:00,275
in the ancestry of people living

31
00:01:00,275 --> 00:01:01,925
far away today.
